Newsreaders typically show (as google does) articles sorted by date
or by thread. But contrary to google (or typical web based "forums"
accessed through web browsers) you don't see all the articles as a
sequence of "paragraphs" lined up, but each single article separately.
That is one reason why context is necessary, and missing context a
nuisance. Then you generally don't have a database archive of posted
articles; news expire on news servers. Differently google, that is
designed to store everything of everyone forever (if not explicitly
marked to expire); you can't even revoke an article with google. So
a reply may arrive at times when the parent article won't even be
available any more, you can't "scroll back" to see the article for
context. Therefore in Usenet it is use to quote sufficient context to
understand an article without scrolling back, need of special tools,
or a good memory.

It's possible to quote context with google; many use google and do so.
You may want to have a look at http://cfaj.freeshell.org/google/ which
explains how to do that (and a bit more).

